Z||000000||BENGAL4||08-15-99||07:54 PM||||Well, I finished my engine detailing project and when I went to start the car, and it wouldn't start. For those of you who might not know, I removed the plenum and rocker arm covers to paint them and in order to remove the rocker covers, I had to remove the intake manifold. Well when i tried to start the car it would just turn over. I checked for fuel and I could smell it so I checked for spark. The coil is okay and I had the ignition module checked and it was okay. Any suggestions? I don't want to tear it back down unless I have to. One note, the car was fine before I did this project. Please help.
Z||000001||BENGAL4||08-16-99||06:31 AM||||One other thing. I had left my drivers side door open all nite and the battery was low, But after charging it, the engine still does not turn over smooth. Meaning that it seems there is an unusual drain on the starting system. The battery is somewhat new so I don't think it's the problem. When I get off work, I am going to have it tested, but if I don't resolve this soon, it will drive me crazy.
Z||000002||Harry||08-16-99||07:18 PM||||Do you mean it would not run?<br>Or does it not crank over at all?
Z||000003||BENGAL4||08-16-99||11:07 PM||||It will not run. I think I located the problem. it is in the distributor. I am going to change the pickup coil tomorrow. When I got home from work today, I tried an experiment.With the wire harness between the ignition coil and the distributor connected, the engine would turn over but it was very erratic and it still wouldn't run. When I removed the harness, it still wouldn't run but it turned over smoothly. I went to the Pontiac dealer and the service manager told me he thought it was the pickup coil or the distributor itself. If anyone has any suggestions, please let me know and I will post the results tomorrow.
Z||000004||BENGAL4||08-17-99||08:34 PM||||Well I replaced the pickup coil and the ingnition module and no change. HELP
