Z||000006||BENGAL4||08-18-99||08:55 AM||||How do you retime it if its not running? This is a new area for me. I know where the timing marks are. But what is strange about this problem is that when anything is connected to the coil, it turns over but its rough. When you disconnect anything, it turns over quick and smooth. ???? 
Z||000007||batboy||08-18-99||10:42 AM||||It sounds like MAYBE your timing is way off. Did you remove the distributor when you were working on detailing the engine? I'm thinking you have to take the distributor out to remove the intake. If you did, then that's probably the problem. If the distributor is ever removed or disturbed, you can't just stick it back in, everything must be aligned correctly.<p>In order to do this, you must have the piston in number 1 cylinder at top dead center and the timing pointer right on the timing mark. Figure out which plug wire goes to number 1 cylinder at the distributor cap. see if the rotor is pointing at that plug wire terminal. If not, then the distributor must be repositioned until it does. This should get timing close enough to start the engine. If you can get it running, you have to set the timing with a timing light,
Z||000008||BENGAL4||08-18-99||11:30 AM||||No, I did not remove the distributor during my detailing project. Right before I started, the car quit on me a mile or two away from home. I pressed on the 2 prong connector that goes into the ignition module and it started right up. I drove it home and that is the last time it ran. I did put a new timing chain and gears on a few weeks ago but had no starting or running problems at that tim either. I know the gears are on correctly because the marks lined up perfectly.  I did remove the distributor to replace the pick up coil and  I installed it according to the marks I made. I have replaced the cap, rotor, wires, plugs, ignition module and pick up coil. Now, how do you tell if the distributor itself is bad? It is the original one and could be bad, but who wants to fork out $100.00 if you don't have to. What puzzles me is, how it turns over differently when wires are attached to the coil and when they are not. When they are attached, it turns over quick and then slow, quick and then slow again. When I removed the wire from the ignition module to the coil, it turns over quickly. If any of you can think of anything else, please let me know. I am going to check the distributor position when I get home and also check the forum to see if yoiu guys can think of anything else. Thanks<p>Bill 
